
  Before I propose that sequence, one question decides the shape of everything below it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what makes a task "change" in a way worth telling someone about?

  - [1mOther people change it.[0m Notification is inherently multi-user — someone reassigns your task, comments, marks it
    done. This needs accounts, a server, and shared storage. It makes the project substantially bigger, and it's the
    layer you cannot bolt on cheaply later: going from single-user local storage to a real backend is close to a rewrite
    of the persistence and state layers.
  - [1mTime changes it.[0m You're the only user; a task becomes notable because it's due tomorrow or it's overdue. This needs
    no server at all — browser storage plus the Notifications API. Far smaller, buildable in this repo as-is, and honest
    about the fact that there's no backend.

  These are cheap in opposite directions. Time-based is cheap to build and cheap to throw away. Multi-user is expensive
  up front but is the thing that's expensive to retrofit — so if you know you want it, it belongs in the foundation, not
  in a later phase.

  My recommendation is [1mtime-based, single-user[0m, unless you actually have collaborators in mind. Nothing about this repo
  suggests a team app, "no backend anywhere" is a strong signal, and a single-user task app with due-date reminders is a
  real, complete, useful product rather than a stunted version of a bigger one. If it grows collaborative later, that's
  a deliberate second project with its own design, not a regret.
